<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lipari</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lipari is the largest of the Aeolian Islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and rich in obsidian, it was settled around 580 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from Cnidus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, whose city of Lipara fought Etruscan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and later sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> took it in 252 BCE during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>, and its main town, also called Lipari, is built around the ancient acropolis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lipari.jpg" alt="Lipari" width="320" height="128" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carsten Steger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_image_of_Lipari_(view_from_the_southwest).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
